{
  "dom_id": "step-q118757",
  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945712/assets/95b534f8c159b37884e32b730d9aa502565ff4971cd7d08903ffbfaa94b76623.png)\n\nConsider $\u25b3PQR$ shown above. The line segment $\\overset{\u2015}{RH}$ is the altitude corresponding to $\\overset{\u2015}{PQ}.$ If $m\u2220PQR={118}^{\u2218},$ calculate $m\u2220QRH.$",
  "worked_solution": "Since the straight angle must have a measure of ${180}^{\u2218},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\u2220HQR+m\u2220PQR & ={180}^{\u2218} \\\\ m\u2220HQR+{118}^{\u2218} & ={180}^{\u2218} \\\\ m\u2220HQR & ={62}^{\u2218}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $\u25b3QHR$ is a right triangle. Since its interior angles must sum to ${180}^{\u2218},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m\u2220QRH+m\u2220HQR+m\u2220RHQ & ={180}^{\u2218} \\\\ m\u2220QRH+{62}^{\u2218}+{90}^{\u2218} & ={180}^{\u2218} \\\\ m\u2220QRH+{152}^{\u2218} & ={180}^{\u2218} \\\\ m\u2220QRH & ={28}^{\u2218}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
